# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository, always.
#
# This repository had no .gitattributes until now, and the result is visible in
# the history: 201 of 216 tracked files on `dev` were stored as CRLF. Once that
# has happened, any commit from a machine configured the other way rewrites
# every line of every file, and a one-line change to .gitignore travels inside
# a diff of several hundred lines where no reviewer will find it. That is not a
# hypothetical here — `config.bat` was added to .gitignore four times.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what goes into the
# repository. Working copies still get native endings on checkout, so nobody
# has to reconfigure their editor.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary

# SVG is text, and is normalised — but see scripts/guard/repo-hygiene.mjs: these
# files legitimately carry very long lines (path data), so the long-line rule
# skips them. The obfuscation-signature rules do not.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Generated — collapsed by default in GitHub's review UI so lockfile churn does
# not drown a real change. Deliberately NOT marked `-diff`: that would make git
# itself treat the file as binary, and `git show` would print "Bin 482073 ->
# 469061 bytes" instead of a diff. A file this guard cannot read is a file a
# payload can sit in. Collapsed-but-expandable, never opaque.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
